<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Modules on cesun.life</title><link>https://example.org/tags/modules/</link><description>Recent content in Modules on cesun.life</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Thu, 19 Feb 2026 04:54:04 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://example.org/tags/modules/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Java Modules</title><link>https://example.org/java-modules/</link><pubDate>Thu, 19 Feb 2026 04:54:04 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/java-modules/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;The Java Platform Module System (JPMS) makes dependencies and package
encapsulation explicit. A named module contains a descriptor,
&lt;code&gt;module-info.java&lt;/code&gt;, at its source root. Compiling it produces
&lt;code&gt;module-info.class&lt;/code&gt; in an exploded module directory or modular JAR.&lt;/p&gt;


